Query speedups over Tantivy: 174.6x at k=1 and 123.9x at k=2. These are exact-output,
same-query comparisons. Tantivy search returns hit objects; the prototype materializes IDs.

Larger English corpus, cache-stress run
---------------------------------------
Dictionary: dwyl/english-words words_alpha.txt
Dictionary words: 370,105
Queries: 100,000 mixed typo/random, seed 42

  k=1 build                          0.226365 s
  k=1 records                        3,774,912 x 4 B = 15,099,648 B
  k=1 directory                      4,194,308 B
  k=1 warm query                     0.031945 s, 108,314 IDs / 433,256 B
  k=2 build                          1.335130 s
  k=2 records                       19,234,970 x 4 B = 76,939,880 B
  k=2 directory                      4,194,308 B
  k=2 warm query                     0.489420 s, 2,448,031 IDs / 9,792,124 B

The 20-bit directory is the measured space/time knee. With 64-bit records, k=2 took
~0.508 s and 153,879,760 record bytes. Packed records therefore reduce persistent record
space by exactly half and improve the cache-stress query by ~3.6%. A 23-bit / 32 MiB
directory improved 64-bit-record k=2 time by only ~3%, so it is not the default.

Methodology caveats
-------------------
- Record/directory bytes exclude the owned dictionary tape, offsets, generation counters,
  and returned IDs. Those must be included in production RSS accounting.
- Timings after the first pass are warm-cache medians. The 100,000-query corpus is included
  to prevent a tiny repeated lookup footprint from making the index look unrealistically fast.
- FastSS is a lossless candidate filter. Every candidate is independently verified, so a
  32-bit residual-hash collision can add work/false candidates before verification but cannot
  change the returned result.
- The prototype's mutable generation array is not thread-safe. Production needs per-call or
  per-worker scratch state before parallel query execution.
